          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Laozi's Background</strong><br>\nLaozi, also known as Lao-Tzu, is believed to be a Chinese philosopher who likely lived during the 6th century BCE. While details about his life are scarce and shrouded in mystery, traditional accounts suggest he was a sage and a keeper of archives at the royal court. His name translates to \"Old Master,\" reflecting the respect and influence his teachings continue to hold.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Tao Te Ching</strong><br>\nLaozi is credited with writing the \"Tao Te Ching,\" a foundational text of Taoism. Comprising around 5,000 Chinese characters, the work is a series of meditations on the mysterious nature of the Tao, or the way of the universe. Its wisdom has guided countless individuals in their search for harmony and understanding of the natural world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Philosophy of Laozi</strong><br>\nLaozi's philosophy centers on the concepts of wu wei (non-action), simplicity, and humility. He emphasizes the importance of yielding and flexibility in life, akin to the suppleness of water, which can overcome the hard and strong. Laozi advocates for a life in accordance with the Tao, leading to peace and serenity.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
